  "name": "Question 14",
  "problem": "Find the root of $f(x)=4{e}^{2x-1}-8.$",
  "worked_solution": "To find the root of $f(x),$  we must solve $f(x)=0:$\n\n$$\n4{e}^{2x-1}-8=0\n$$\n\nFirst, we isolate the term with the variable exponent by adding $8$ to both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{e}^{2x-1}-8 & =0 \\\\ 4{e}^{2x-1}-\\cancel{8}+\\cancel{8} & =0+8 \\\\ 4{e}^{2x-1} & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing both sides by $4,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{e}^{2x-1} & =8 \\\\ {e}^{2x-1} & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThen we take $ln$ of both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2x-1} & =2 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}\u2061({e}^{2x-1}) & =\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(2) \\\\ 2x-1 & =\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-1 & =\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(2) \\\\ 2x & =\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(2)+1 \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(2)+1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
